//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="total=20 same=true\nbefore\nthe quick brown fox\nafter zero=0\nat_end=eof closed=io forged_src=io forged_dst=io\ncleaned=true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.copy, os.fs.std, err.union, err.propagate
//!
//! s200 (wolf-lang#417, `[os.fs.copy]`) — `fs_copy_chunk(src, dst, max)`:
//! one transfer of at most `max` bytes from `src`'s offset to `dst`'s,
//! both advancing, the bytes never passing through the program (on
//! linux, never through user space: `copy_file_range`, `sendfile`,
//! `splice`, the read/write loop last). HOW MANY bytes one call moves is
//! the host's, so this file pins totals, never a split:
//!
//!   total / same     a 20-byte file copied 7 bytes at most per call to
//!                    a new file: 20 moved, the copy byte-identical.
//!   before / after   the same file onto descriptor 1, between `print`s:
//!                    in program order with them, on every tier.
//!   zero             a `max` of zero moves nothing and answers 0.
//!   at_end           a source at its end is `eof`.
//!   closed / forged  either handle closed or forged is `io`.
